WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.294
Authentication answers the question, who
is connected?

2
00:00:06.374 --> 00:00:08.909
In ASP.NET Core SignalR,

3
00:00:08.989 --> 00:00:17.076
the hub can integrate with the same
authentication system used by the rest of the

4
00:00:17.156 --> 00:00:18.604
application.

5
00:00:18.684 --> 00:00:22.185
When a connection is established,

6
00:00:22.265 --> 00:00:28.783
the server can associate a user principal
with that connection,

7
00:00:28.863 --> 00:00:35.622
and hub code can make decisions using the
authenticated identity.

8
00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:42.220
The details matter because SignalR clients
are not all the same.

9
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.680
Browser clients may use cookies.

10
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.312
Browser or non-browser clients may use
bearer tokens.

11
00:00:51.392 --> 00:00:59.962
Mobile, desktop, service, and generated
clients may have different token storage,

12
00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:03.059
refresh, and retry behavior.

13
00:01:03.139 --> 00:01:09.899
A secure design documents which credential
type each client uses,

14
00:01:09.979 --> 00:01:13.600
how the connection is established,

15
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.353
and how authentication failure appears to
that client.

16
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:25.106
A long-lived connection also changes the
timing model.

17
00:01:25.186 --> 00:01:32.549
Authentication data is typically captured
when the connection starts.

18
00:01:32.629 --> 00:01:40.475
It should not be assumed to update
instantly while the connection stays open.

19
00:01:40.555 --> 00:01:47.314
Token expiration, user logout, disabled
accounts, role changes,

20
00:01:47.394 --> 00:01:50.653
claim updates, tenant changes,

21
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:58.458
and permission changes all need deliberate
connection-lifecycle review.

22
00:01:58.538 --> 00:02:04.211
That review can lead to different
engineering choices.

23
00:02:04.291 --> 00:02:10.326
Some applications require shorter
connection lifetimes.

24
00:02:10.406 --> 00:02:16.562
Some close connections when authentication
state changes.

25
00:02:16.642 --> 00:02:21.350
Some require reconnects to pick up new
claims.

26
00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:30.241
Some avoid placing highly dynamic
authorization facts in long-lived identity claims

27
00:02:30.321 --> 00:02:36.718
and instead check trusted server-side data
when a method runs.

28
00:02:36.798 --> 00:02:40.902
The important point is predictability.

29
00:02:40.982 --> 00:02:49.552
Authenticated SignalR connections should
behave consistently across browser and

30
00:02:49.632 --> 00:02:51.926
non-browser clients.

31
00:02:52.006 --> 00:02:58.765
Failed authentication should produce
understandable outcomes,

32
00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:07.174
and the server should never assume that a
connected client remains authorized for

33
00:03:07.254 --> 00:03:15.220
every future action just because an
earlier connection handshake succeeded.
